stokes: Then we'll pick up with the rest.

671
01:30:08.920 --> 01:30:10.030
stokes: On the next call.

672
01:30:10.630 --> 01:30:16.040
stokes: So, yeah, I guess any… any brief comments, for this, or…

673
01:30:19.390 --> 01:30:21.400
stokes: Lukasz, you can go ahead.

674
01:30:25.250 --> 01:30:35.210
Łukasz Rozmej: Okay, I have 2 problems with the CIP. So to give a bit more context, I love the idea of execution proofs.

675
01:30:35.250 --> 01:30:53.879
Łukasz Rozmej: I am working on a Nethermind guest client that would support creating execution proofs in zkVMs. We already have support for 3 different zkVMs, and currently I'm optimizing it, so just to give you the context.

676
01:30:53.880 --> 01:31:01.399
Łukasz Rozmej: So, I love the whole concept, but I'm… I'm not sure if I can get behind, CFI-ing this.

677
01:31:01.420 --> 01:31:11.369
Łukasz Rozmej: with 2 to be major problems. One problem is that historically, when we ship something in in the fork, it's ready for productional use.

678
01:31:11.440 --> 01:31:29.140
Łukasz Rozmej: even if it's optional, this won't be actually secure enough due to, for example, gas repricing that will need to happen for this to actually be secure. So we'll ship something that we know that isn't secure enough, and that users shouldn't actually use it, so

679
01:31:29.140 --> 01:31:32.350
Łukasz Rozmej: I don't see the point of shipping it in the fork.

680
01:31:32.490 --> 01:31:46.679
Łukasz Rozmej: That's one thing, right? So, we are giving mixed signals to the community. Okay, we ship something, but it's not really production ready, so this is… this is wrong to me, this is not what's…

681
01:31:46.710 --> 01:31:50.329
Łukasz Rozmej: We should be putting into the the fork.

682
01:31:50.360 --> 01:32:10.139
Łukasz Rozmej: And second problem is that I think with the current solution, unless it's changed, the, pairs of guest programs and zkVMs are kind of chosen by committee. So historically, anyone could write.

683
01:32:10.250 --> 01:32:21.410
Łukasz Rozmej: protocol implementations of clients, and they could compete on their technical prowess, for example, security, stability, and technical prowess.

684
01:32:21.520 --> 01:32:38.060
Łukasz Rozmej: Here, there will be some decision made up front, and some clients and VM pairs will be embedded into protocol. Which, okay, they can change later, but this is a process, so this kind of.

685
01:32:38.380 --> 01:32:53.550
Łukasz Rozmej: breaks the current, like, culture of creating Ethereum software that I have a problem with. So those are my two problems with the EIP. And I love execution proofs, so…

686
01:32:53.700 --> 01:32:55.960
Łukasz Rozmej: Just, just, just saying that.

687
01:32:58.820 --> 01:33:03.970
stokes: Okay. Yeah. Ignacio, let's just take, your comments, and we'll wrap up.

688
01:33:04.120 --> 01:33:06.479
stokes: Yeah, just to be clear here.

689
01:33:06.600 --> 01:33:11.170
stokes: We will pick this one back up. We will not make a decision today. We just are at time.

690
01:33:12.200 --> 01:33:23.760
ignacio: Yeah, I just wanted to touch on Lukasz's comments. I think many of his concerns are more related to mandatory proofs than to EIP-8025, which is optional proofs.

691
01:33:23.890 --> 01:33:34.389
ignacio: Ad25 is not really committing to any kind of pairing of guest programs on ZKVMs or enshrining any kind of proof system or anything like that.

692
01:33:34.630 --> 01:33:40.809
ignacio: It's mainly about defining the specs and upstreaming this work into.

693
01:33:41.540 --> 01:34:01.369
ignacio: a repository where we can settle a lot of work that we have done, that we know it is useful, and we can defer all this discussion about, okay, which guest programs, which ZKVMs, and all that to mandatory proofs until we have more data, and we know what is safe.

694
01:34:01.380 --> 01:34:07.329
ignacio: The main idea for us, on why we think EIP-8025 is important to

695
01:34:07.550 --> 01:34:18.930
ignacio: to be included in the specs is that it will de-risk a lot of analysis on how zkVMs behave on other protocol changes, such as PeerDAS.

696
01:34:19.070 --> 01:34:20.010
ignacio: Umm.

697
01:34:20.140 --> 01:34:29.209
ignacio: If we don't do this, we have to maintain, like, a zkVM branch on the specs, PVT branch on the specs, we have to mix both, and this is…

698
01:34:29.440 --> 01:34:34.109
ignacio: Increasingly inefficient, considering that we have limited resources also to work.

699
01:34:34.400 --> 01:34:36.890
ignacio: on many other important things for Ethereum.

700
01:34:37.040 --> 01:34:47.929
ignacio: So that's kind of a short answer to these points, but I'm happy to keep talking about this in the next call, if needed.

701
01:34:47.930 --> 01:34:54.369
Łukasz Rozmej: So would you consider this an informational EIP? Basically, it's defining the spec, but not shipping anything.

702
01:34:55.830 --> 01:34:58.920
ignacio: Well, I already know if information on the EIPs means that

703
01:34:59.140 --> 01:35:03.830
ignacio: will get merged into these specs. Maybe I'm not…

704
01:35:03.970 --> 01:35:08.229
ignacio: Fully aware how precise the process is on this front.

705
01:35:10.270 --> 01:35:16.299
stokes: Just to jump in here, we are at time. Are we okay just to hold this conversation until the next call?

706
01:35:20.200 --> 01:35:21.070
stokes: Okay.

707
01:35:21.660 --> 01:35:35.259
stokes: Okay. Lots of thumbs up. Okay. Cool. Then, yeah, thanks, everyone. We'll keep working through these EIPs on the Hegota scope, next time. And, yeah, again.

708
01:35:35.390 --> 01:35:40.399
stokes: Let's all keep an eye on Sepolia. The fork will happen in just a few days.

709
01:35:40.580 --> 01:35:45.140
stokes: So… Happy forking, and I'll see you next time. Thank you.

710
01:35:46.180 --> 01:35:47.070
potuz: Bye-bye.

711
01:35:49.750 --> 01:35:50.460
Barnabas: Okay.

